<section class="section">
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">Who this is for</p>
    <h2>A better rate is only half the question.</h2>
    <p>Refinancing gets sold as "cut your rate." Sometimes that's the whole job. Often the more useful work is fixing the structure you set up years ago. splitting a loan, adding an offset, releasing equity, or getting off a package that no longer earns its fee.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You haven't compared your rate to the market in a couple of years</li>
      <li>A fixed term is ending and you don't want to roll into whatever the bank offers</li>
      <li>You want cash out for renovations, an investment, or the next purchase</li>
      <li>The current loan is fine on rate and wrong on features</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--cool">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="container--narrow" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-6); padding-inline: 0;">
      <p class="eyebrow">How I help</p>
      <h2>Numbers first. Then a recommendation.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="grid grid--3">
      <article class="card">
        <h3>The break-even</h3>
        <p>Discharge fees, any break costs, application fees, and how many months of saving it takes to get them back. If the payback is ugly, I'll say stay. I have a <a href="/calculators/refinance/">refinance calculator</a> if you want a first pass.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>More than the headline rate</h3>
        <p>Offset vs basic, split loans, redraw traps, and whether your current lender will match to keep you. A 0.10% cut that loses a useful offset is not a win.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>I don't disappear after</h3>
        <p>If we move the loan, I review it every year against the market. Most years the answer is "it's still fine." Some years it isn't. Either way you'll know.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

